From 2a176f5e271d251a3b5bcdae2df22bfc0ce2d2a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:16:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 117/269] gallium/pvr: run pco for real - the driver now
 compiles shaders

The compiler that has been linked since the skeleton round is now actually run.
A NIR shader goes through the full pco pipeline and comes out as encoded USC
microcode:

  runtime: free list 102400..2147483648 bytes, shared 11264, coeffs 4096,
           phantoms 1, cores 1
  shader:  pco encoded 224 bytes of USC for a fragment shader
  shader:  a three-store shader encodes 232 bytes (vs 224)

The second line is what makes the first one evidence rather than a number: a
shader that does strictly more work encodes strictly more code. A single non-zero
size could have been a constant.

In support of that:

* DRM_PVR_DEV_QUERY_RUNTIME_INFO_GET, which pco_ctx_create needs and the driver did
  not have. Translated the same way the Vulkan winsys does. core_count is pinned to
  1 to match it - the kernel has no query for it, and inventing one would be worse
  than agreeing with the reference.
* The pco context is created once per screen with the USC library, a blob generated
  at build time from pco/usclib's OpenCL sources. libpowervr_uscgen is linked for it.
* pvr_shader_compile() runs preprocess, lower, postprocess, translate, process,
  encode - copied from the Vulkan driver's build_shader() because the order is
  load-bearing: getting it wrong yields a plausible binary rather than an error.

Runnable check extended: the probe now reports the runtime info and compiles two
fragment shaders, requiring the larger one to produce more bytes. Verified on the
real open kernel module, ALL PASS.

What this is NOT: nothing calls this from a GL draw yet. There is no create_vs_state
or create_fs_state, no shader caps declared (so GL still reports 1.5/ES-CM 1.1 and
exposes no shader stage), no pipeline state and no job submission. Compiling a shader
is a prerequisite for drawing, not drawing.
